// HUD v2 · type-check for the E2E lane. The root tsconfig.json has
// `include: ["src"]`, so e2e/*.spec.ts and playwright.config.ts are compiled by
// NOTHING: `npm run typecheck` skips them by include, and e2e.yml has no
// `pull_request` trigger (see its header), so the only thing that ever parses
// them is Playwright's own loader — on the nightly, after the merge.
//
// The gap is not hypothetical. While this lane was being worked on, a block
// comment in a spec containing the glob `**/chat/stream` closed itself early on
// the `*/`, turning the rest of the line into code. `tsc --noEmit` exited 0 on
// that tree and Playwright's loader was what caught it. It never reached a
// commit — it was fixed in the working tree — so nothing shipped; what the gap
// cost was that no gate could have told anyone.
//
// What this catches: parse errors, unresolved names, and misuse of a typed API
// (`page.goto(42)` is TS2345). What it does NOT catch: the root config's
// `strict: false` / `noImplicitAny: false` are inherited, so `const s: string =
// null` and implicit-any chains pass, and a `// @ts-nocheck` header opts a spec
// out entirely. It is a syntax-and-signature gate, not a strictness gate.
//
// Separate file rather than widening the root include: the specs need Node's
// globals (`process`, `node:fs`) and the app must not get them. Measured, so
// the next reader does not have to re-derive it: putting `types: ["node"]` on
// the root config makes `process.env.X` type-check inside src/ at exit 0, with
// nothing supplying `process` in the served bundle (`grep -ro 'process\.env'
// agents/web/v2/` returns nothing). src/ is not free of `process` today — six
// files under src/test/ call `process.cwd()` — but every one of them carries a
// `// @ts-nocheck` header, so the app gate is green over them by opt-out, not
// by absence. The root config now pins `types: []` so this isolation is a
// stated property rather than a TypeScript default that a version bump could
// change.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": { "types": ["node"] },
  // `vite.config.ts` is here for the same reason `playwright.config.ts` is: the root config's
  // `include: ["src"]` does not cover it, so nothing compiled it and it had been carrying a real
  // TS2769 (`test` not in `UserConfigExport`) that no gate could see. Why *this* program and not
  // the app's runs opposite to the obvious reading, so it is measured rather than argued: this
  // file does not consume the `types: ["node"]` above, it supplies node types to everything
  // compiled beside it. `vite/dist/node/index.d.ts` opens with a program-wide
  // `/// <reference types="node" />`, so importing `vite`/`vitest/config` anywhere hands
  // @types/node globals to every file in the program. Compiling `vite.config.ts` by itself under
  // the root config's `types: []` exits 0; adding it to the root `include: ["src"]` makes a bare
  // `process.env.FOO` inside `src/` type-check at exit 0 — the leak that `types: []` exists to
  // stop. `types: ["node"]` above therefore stays for the specs, whose own need for `process` and
  // `node:fs` should be a stated property of this config and not something inherited from
  // whatever a vite `.d.ts` happens to reference.
  "include": ["e2e", "playwright.config.ts", "vite.config.ts"],
  // gitignored Playwright output; it lands inside the include glob after a
  // local `npm run e2e` and would red a developer's gate on artifacts.
  "exclude": ["e2e/artifacts", "e2e/.results"]
}
